    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d530">

                <pb facs="0796" n="664" xml:id="pg664"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/7978: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >MacMurray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1926-12-28T10:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">December 28,
                                1926—10 a.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received December 28—4: 58
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>642. 1. I earnestly recommend that British, French, Italian, and Japanese
                    Governments be informed by the Department of its decision as stated in the final
                    sentence of your telegram No. 307, December 23, 11 a.m.</p>
                <p>2. There seems to be a prime necessity for this action, both because the other
                    powers with naval forces at Shanghai would seem to be entitled to know the
                    attitude of the United States and because if they are not informed there is the
                    possibility that our Government might be criticized should the other interested
                    powers consider it expedient to take certain measures of defense in a sudden
                    emergency in order to protect the integrity of the Settlement in a way in which
                    our Government would not be ready to cooperate.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">MacMurray</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
